[{:seon.task/name "a-recorded-read-sees-every-read-with-the-cache-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Root, from the owner, 2026-11-14 (\"we shouldn't just accept the bad ideas that are easily fixable\"); deps' first item after the fork bound. What exists: datahike.reads/with-reads records each index range a read scans (di/record-read!, 9 sites in the persistent-set index protocol), but it sees every read only by switching off what skips that protocol, all added in Datahike 9a0f3c9c: the query planner (q/*disable-planner*), the q result cache (q/*query-result-cache?*), the search memo (search.cljc memoize-for), and a lazy read realized after with-reads returns goes unrecorded. Seon's ctx/wire.clj:506 unbinds the recorder for that reason. Measured on root's live db [M]: q over 680 tasks 10 us from the result cache, 1,158 us recording (x114); datoms +0.9 us; pull no measurable change. Change, each red first: (1) the planner's cursors call .lookupGE/.seekGE on PersistentSortedSet (query/execute.cljc:89, :215) with no record-read!: record at those seeks; (2) a q result-cache hit replays the ranges recorded when the result was computed, kept with the cached value; (3) the same for a search-memo hit; (4) a lazy read captures the recorder atom at its creation, so realizing it later records into the read it belongs to. Deletes: the three switch-offs in with-reads and search.cljc's (some? *reads*) bypass, and wire.clj's unbinding. Estimate: a recorded q from the cache within 2x of the unrecorded 10 us (one replay of its ranges, a conj each); measured before and after on root's db."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.db-test/a-recorded-read-sees-every-read-with-the-cache-on}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}]
